Neutron#53 (@atsuenn): Premiere Pro 2026 on an Intel Arc B390 (Panther Lake, Framework Laptop 13 Pro, xe, Mesa 26.2.6,
intel-compute-runtime) -- "as SOON as i open it, i get a System Compatibility report" ("No graphics processor suitable
for GPU-accelerated render"). On Windows the Arc is used.

d3d11: answer Intel's "Intel Device Information" counter on Intel adapters (kill switch NEUTRON_DXVK_INTEL_DEVINFO=0)

Mechanism (SOLID, lab + static reading of GPUSniffer.exe 26.5.2.5): Premiere's GPU check (GPUSniffer, also run by After
Effects and Media Encoder) lists Intel's OpenCL device ("No capability restrictions detected - adding device to list") but
only initializes it after Intel's own device query succeeds -- a D3D11 device-dependent counter that Intel's Windows driver
exposes. On an AMD device the equivalent is cl_amd_device_attribute_query (lab control from i53b: with it 545, without it
14, the same "isn't initialized"). DXVK reported no device-dependent counters (CheckCounterInfo LastDeviceDependentCounter
0, CheckCounter/CreateCounter E_INVALIDARG), so the device stayed "* Not chosen because it isn't initialized." and
GPUSniffer returned 14 -- the reporter's result, before and after 187.1's driver-version fix.

What GPUSniffer does (static reading, located for this fix; same protocol as Intel's public GPU Detect sample,
github.com/GameTechDev/gpudetect GPUDetect.cpp GetIntelDeviceInfo):
- only when the DirectX-key driver build is 5103..65535 (Intel's numbering; 187.1 made that key real);
- CheckCounterInfo, then CheckCounter twice per device-dependent counter (lengths, then strings); the counter whose name is
  "Intel Device Information" (exactly 24 characters) is Intel's;
- version = sscanf(description, "Version %d"); no match -> version 1, 8 bytes. Then sscanf(units, "Size %d"): no match ->
  24 bytes if version >= 2; a number -> that size; EMPTY units -> size 0 -> counter rejected (lab-confirmed below);
- CreateCounter, Begin, End, GetData(8 bytes) must return exactly S_OK; the 8 bytes are the ADDRESS of the driver's block,
  which it copies; the version must be 2 ("invalid intel device info header version, expect: 2");
- block = IntelDeviceInfoV2 { GPUMaxFreq, GPUMinFreq, GTGeneration, EUCount, PackageTDP, MaxFillRate } (DWORDs, 24
  bytes); it reports "Hardware Family" = GTGeneration and keeps GTGeneration and EUCount (its compute-unit gate drops from 20
  to 16 for GTGeneration >= 0x21, Tiger Lake).
Our first prototype (i53b) returned an empty description: GPUSniffer read version 1 and rejected it.

Windows/Intel behavior this mirrors: Intel's D3D11 driver exposes one device-dependent counter "Intel Device Information",
description "Version 2", whose GetData returns a pointer to driver-owned IntelDeviceInfoV2 (gpudetect GPUDetect.cpp, the
struct and the "Version %d" parse; DeviceId.h INTEL_GPU_ARCHITECTURE = the values GTGeneration carries, asserted against
the device-id table in GPUDetect.cpp InitCounterInfo). This patch exposes exactly that counter, ONLY when the Vulkan
adapter's vendor is Intel (0x8086), and fills it from the real hardware:
- GTGeneration: Intel's PRODUCT_FAMILY for the PCI device id the app sees through DXGI. Up to Alchemist: Intel's GPU
  Detect table (gpudetect DeviceId.cpp GetIntelGPUArchitecture; Tiger Lake 0x21, Rocket Lake 0x23, Alder Lake 0x24, DG1
  1210, Alchemist 1270). Newer: ids from the Linux kernel's include/drm/intel/pciids.h (INTEL_PTL/LNL/BMG/ARL/MTL_IDS),
  values from Intel's igfxfmid.h (gmmlib Source/inc/common/igfxfmid.h, intel-graphics-compiler inc/common/igfxfmid.h):
  Meteor Lake 1272, Arrow Lake 1273, Battlemage 1274, Lunar Lake 1275, Panther Lake 1300 (the reporter's B390 is 0xB082).
  igfxfmid.h and gpudetect agree on DG1 1210 and Alchemist 1270; their low numbers differ, and gpudetect's (Windows) win.
- EUCount: CL_DEVICE_MAX_COMPUTE_UNITS of Intel's own OpenCL runtime (compute-runtime/NEO, platform vendor "Intel(R)
  Corporation", device vendor 0x8086) for the same PCI address (cl_khr_pci_bus_info vs VK_EXT_pci_bus_info; the only Intel
  OpenCL GPU when one side has no PCI info). NEO sets that value to GT_SYSTEM_INFO.EUCount, the fused EU count from the
  kernel driver (compute-runtime shared/source/device/device_caps.cpp). Vulkan and sysfs do not report EUs and a PE DLL
  cannot issue the xe/i915 topology ioctl, so Intel's runtime is the source DXVK can reach -- and Premiere needs that
  runtime installed anyway (it refuses Mesa's OpenCL by vendor). Read on the first CreateCounter, not at device creation.
- GPUMaxFreq/GPUMinFreq: the GPU's hardware RP0/RPn from sysfs, found by PCI slot (/sys/class/drm/cardN/device/uevent):
  xe device/tile0/gt0/freq0/rp0_freq + rpn_freq, i915 gt_RP0_freq_mhz + gt_RPn_freq_mhz; max falls back to NEO's
  CL_DEVICE_MAX_CLOCK_FREQUENCY.
- units "Size 24" (GPUSniffer needs non-empty units; with "Size 24" or any other non-"Size" text it reads 24 bytes).
One info line per adapter goes to the DXVK log: "D3D11: Intel Device Information v2 for device 0x...: GTGeneration ...,
EUCount ... (source), GPUMaxFreq ... (source)".

Not covered / INFERRED:
- Never run on real Intel hardware. The exact units text and counter type Intel's driver reports are not public; GPUSniffer
  and Intel's sample behave the same for any non-empty units. Whether Intel's Windows driver reports Panther Lake as 1300
  is INFERRED (igfxfmid.h; the high block agrees with the Windows-read values for DG1/Alchemist).
- PackageTDP and MaxFillRate are 0 (no user-space source; GPUSniffer prints them and keeps neither).
- EUCount is 0 without Intel's OpenCL runtime (then Premiere has no Intel OpenCL device to initialize anyway).
- Wildcat Lake (0xFD80/FD81), Nova Lake and Raptor Lake ids have no table entry (GTGeneration 0 = IGFX_UNKNOWN).
- x64 d3d11 only (the x86 DLLs are dxvk-stock); D3D10's counter calls stay unimplemented as upstream.
- After init GPUSniffer still checks driver blocklist, compute units and memory on the real Arc; the lab passes them
  with an NVIDIA GPU underneath.

Lab evidence (runtime 11.10-205 + this d3d11 built from DXVK 3c6f5083 + patches/dxvk 0001-0008 as in 205, NVIDIA RTX 5070,
prefix reflink of a Premiere 2026 lab prefix, desk i53c; rig = the NVIDIA GPU presented as an Arc B390: DXVK
customVendorId 8086 / customDeviceId b082, NVIDIA's OpenCL renamed to Intel's runtime by an LD_PRELOAD shim, CUDA hidden,
NEUTRON_DXKEY_DRIVER_VERSION=0; NEUTRON_DXVK_INTEL_DEVINFO_ANY_GPU=1 lets the code run on it):
- GPUSniffer kill-switch A/B on the same DLL, n=3 each: counter on -> device initialized ("Vendor: Intel", "Hardware Family:
  1300"), clCreateContext, DirectX test passes, result 545(0x221) (s-on1..3); NEUTRON_DXVK_INTEL_DEVINFO=0 -> "* Not
  chosen because it isn't initialized.", no context, 14(0xE) (s-off1..3). 545 is also i53b's AMD positive control.
  Shipped 205 d3d11 on the same rig: 14 (s-205a). Without ANY_GPU (real vendor NVIDIA, DXGI still saying Intel): 14, no
  counter (s-noany1) -- the gate reads the real adapter.
- Empty-units variant (lab build): GPUSniffer 14, harness parses size 0 (s-nounits1, h-nounits1).
- Mingw harness doing Intel's sample + GPUSniffer's parse: name "Intel Device Information" (25), units "Size 24", desc
  "Version 2", header 24/2, GetDataSize 8, GetData(NULL,0) S_OK, block GTGeneration 0x514 (1300), EUCount 48 (the NVIDIA
  device's compute units through the shim, matched by PCI 0:1:0.0), GPUMaxFreq 2512 from OpenCL; sysfs card found by PCI
  slot (/sys/class/drm/card1, no xe/i915 files on NVIDIA). Lab-only fake sysfs roots: xe 2500/400, i915 2100/300 read.
- Plain NVIDIA adapter: CheckCounterInfo 0, "no device dependent counters" (settle-nv).
- Regression on NVIDIA (no rig): Premiere 2026 home screen identical with this DLL (n=3) and 205's (n=1); Photoshop 2026
  home + sign-in dialog identical with this DLL (n=2) and 205's (n=1); no "Intel Device Information" line in any app log.

Kill switch: NEUTRON_DXVK_INTEL_DEVINFO=0 (no counters, upstream behavior). Development only:
NEUTRON_DXVK_INTEL_DEVINFO_ANY_GPU=1 exposes it on any GPU (lab rigs; same idea as NEUTRON_AMF_ANY_GPU).
